 comments:[
  {path:'packages/eslint-config/obsidian-scanner.upstream.js',line:1,side:'RIGHT',body:'**Vendored, not written.** This file is the exact output of \`buildScannerEslintConfig()\` in [obsidian-workflows at 8167cae](https://github.com/obsidianmd/obsidian-workflows/blob/8167caed39664214d82c86fdfa32e06d8d55f61d/src/lint.ts#L544-L697), generated from their source rather than retyped, and byte-identical to it. Review it by comparing against upstream, not line by line.\n\nTo update: regenerate from the new upstream commit and replace the file, then update the commit in the header. Never hand-edit it; local changes belong in \`obsidian-scanner.js\`. The MIT notice is required by the license, not decoration.'},
  {path:'packages/eslint-config/obsidian-scanner.upstream.js',line:35,side:'RIGHT',body:'**The severity model that drives everything else.** \`toWarns\` downgrades every rule in obsidianmd\\'s \`recommended\` set to a warning, except \`eslint-comments/*\`. The blocks below then re-escalate a specific list back to error. Only those errors block a submission.\n\nSo \`error\` in \`recommended\` does not mean blocking. \`obsidianmd/rule-custom-message\`, which reports \`no-console\`, is error-level in \`recommended\` but isn\\'t re-escalated, so the scanner reports it as a warning.\n\nThe \`eslint-comments\` exemption is why 38 of the 67 blocking errors on main come from four comment-hygiene rules.'},
  {path:'packages/eslint-config/obsidian-scanner.upstream.js',line:67,side:'RIGHT',body:'\`tsconfigRootDir: cwd()\` makes the config depend on the process working directory. It must run from \`apps/obsidian\`: the CI step uses \`pnpm --dir apps/obsidian exec\`, the \`lint:scanner\` script runs there, and the test runs under vitest whose cwd is the app. Running it from the repo root breaks TypeScript project resolution.'},
  {path:'packages/eslint-config/obsidian-scanner.upstream.js',line:137,side:'RIGHT',body:'**This block plus the security rules above it are the definition of \"blocks a submission\".** \`errorRules\` in \`obsidian-scanner.js\` repeats this list on purpose, because the editor needs it as data. \`apps/obsidian/test/scannerConfig.test.mts\` fails if the two disagree in either direction, including when a re-vendor adds an escalation we haven\\'t listed. I verified that case by simulating one.'},
  {path:'packages/eslint-config/obsidian-scanner.js',line:6,side:'RIGHT',body:'The boundary: everything the scanner runs comes from the vendored file. Everything after this line is ours, and exists only because upstream has no equivalent: rules for the editor and the test.'},
  {path:'packages/eslint-config/obsidian-scanner.js',line:15,side:'RIGHT',body:'Derived from \`recommended\` at runtime rather than listed. An earlier hand-written version hardcoded \`no-restricted-disable\` with 2 banned rules; upstream bans 12. Disabling \`@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any\`, for example, would have passed here and failed the real scan. The \`throw\` below makes an upstream rename fail loudly instead of silently dropping these rules from the editor.'},
  {path:'packages/eslint-config/obsidian-scanner.js',line:28,side:'RIGHT',body:'Adding a rule here does **not** make it block. Blocking is decided by the vendored config. If you add a rule here that upstream doesn\\'t escalate, the test fails. To change what blocks, re-vendor a newer upstream version; this list then has to follow, and the test tells you what\\'s missing.'},
  {path:'packages/eslint-config/obsidian-scanner.js',line:67,side:'RIGHT',body:'**Why the editor sees less than the scanner.** \`editorRules\` is \`errorRules\` minus rules whose plugin \`apps/obsidian/eslint.config.mjs\` can\\'t resolve: \`no-unsanitized/*\` isn\\'t a dependency there, and registering a rule without its plugin is a hard ESLint failure.\n\nKnown limitation: the editor only shows rules that block. Warn-level scanner findings, including \`no-console\`, never get a squiggle; only \`lint:scanner\` shows the full set.'},
  {path:'.prettierignore',line:3,side:'RIGHT',body:'Keeps the vendored config byte-identical to upstream, so a future update is a plain text diff. \`format-changed-files\` respects this file, and so does the commit hook (verified: the hook left the file untouched).'}
 ]
};
